\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Positivity of one step}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{The \vocabk{normal transfer}{fdip-01BF} is self-adjoint
whenever it exists. On a fixed slice it exists and is strictly
positive exactly when
##{1-a_sM+a_s\lambda_{\min}(W_l^{\rm W})>0.}
For every unitary transverse connection this holds if #{a_sM<1}.
At #{a_sM=1} the step is strictly positive exactly when the
transverse shifts have no nonzero common fixed field.}

\proof{
\p{Suppress #{l} and write #{t=a_s}. The two equations defining
#{T} give its quadratic form on a pair:
##{\langle (u,v),T(u,v)\rangle=
\langle u-tEv,B_+^{-1}(u-tEv)\rangle
+\langle v,B_-v\rangle .}
Direct substitution also gives adjoint cross terms, hence
self-adjointness. The shear #{(u,v)\mapsto(u-tEv,v)} is
invertible. Positivity is therefore equivalent to positivity
of both restrictions of #{\bbI+t(W_l^{\rm W}-M\bbI)}.
When both are positive the defining inverse exists.
For each unitary shift #{S}, #{2\bbI -S-S^\dagger=
(\bbI -S)^\dagger(\bbI -S)}. Hence #{W_l^{\rm W}\ge0}
and its kernel is exactly the common fixed space. These facts
give the asserted criterion.}

\p{At trivial links a transverse constant is fixed. Thus
at #{a_sM=1} the positive-normal pivot is singular, and at
#{a_sM>1} these links cannot give a positive step.
A transverse circle of period one with shift #{-\bbI}, on
the other hand, has #{W_l^{\rm W}=2\bbI /a}. At equal
spacings it gives a positive step for #{1\le a_sM<2}.}
}
